\f0\b0  Because you process crises with cool detachment, you may pivot to solutions while your team is still processing the emotional fallout of a setback. 
\f2\i Observable behavior:
\f0\i0  Cutting off venting sessions prematurely with "Let's focus on next steps."\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Over-Indexing on Utility Over Connection:
\f0\b0  Your strategic mind views interactions through the lens of efficiency. You might inadvertently treat people as pieces on a chessboard\'97even if you are moving them toward a genuinely good outcome. 
\f2\i Observable behavior:
\f0\i0  Skipping relationship-building small talk and jumping straight into transactional requests.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The "Calculated Transparency" Trap:
\f0\b0  You likely curate what you share to achieve specific reactions. While often effective, highly perceptive team members may sense this and feel managed rather than trusted. 
\f2\i Observable behavior:
\f0\i0  Framing decisions in ways that emphasize only what you think the listener wants to hear, rather than the whole picture.\uc0\u8232 \
